WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.504
The story of the Suez Canal is a story of determination.

2
00:00:04.505 --> 00:00:11.685
It started as a simple idea, turned into a grand dream, and finally became a reality.

3
00:00:11.686 --> 00:00:18.414
The man behind this dream was a French diplomat named Ferdinand de Lesseps.

4
00:00:18.415 --> 00:00:25.576
Born in France in 1805, Ferdinand was a bright, young man with a promising future.

5
00:00:25.577 --> 00:00:30.516
His father was a diplomat who worked in many countries around the world.

6
00:00:30.517 --> 00:00:34.095
Ferdinand studied to become a diplomat like his father,

7
00:00:34.096 --> 00:00:42.442
and in 1832 he was sent to Egypt to take a diplomatic job as the French consul at Alexandria.

8
00:00:42.443 --> 00:00:50.880
Upon his arrival in the ancient city, however, Ferdinand had to stay in a hospital because someone from his ship had fallen sick.

9
00:00:50.881 --> 00:00:59.410
While he and others from the ship waited to learn if they were sick too, Ferdinand grew restless and picked up a book to read.

10
00:00:59.411 --> 00:01:09.869
The book was a report written by a French engineer, and reading it inspired a dream that Ferdinand pursued for the rest of his life.

11
00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:16.012
The report was about building a waterway between the Mediterranean and Red seas.

12
00:01:16.013 --> 00:01:23.828
At that time ships sailing between Europe and Asia had to travel around the entire continent of Africa.

13
00:01:23.829 --> 00:01:31.094
If built, this new route would greatly shorten the trip between Europe and the Far East.

14
00:01:31.095 --> 00:01:41.096
The report stated that building a canal from the Mediterranean to the Red Sea was impossible because of the different water levels of the two seas.

15
00:01:41.097 --> 00:01:44.516
But this conclusion did not discourage Ferdinand.

16
00:01:44.517 --> 00:01:49.139
Instead, as soon as the young consul was released from the hospital,

17
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:57.010
he began researching the narrow piece of land, called the Isthmus of Suez, that separated the two seas.

18
00:01:57.011 --> 00:02:00.236
Ferdinand discovered that the pharaohs of Ancient Egypt

19
00:02:00.237 --> 00:02:09.628
had built waterways to connect the Nile River to the Red Sea to have access to the rich copper mines and spice lands of East Africa.

20
00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:16.821
Unfortunately, this was thousands of years in the past, and those waterways were long lost.

21
00:02:16.822 --> 00:02:26.626
But now that he knew a similar canal had once been built, Ferdinand was more determined than ever to pursue his goal.

22
00:02:26.627 --> 00:02:30.903
Ferdinand de Lesseps' duties as the French consul were demanding.

23
00:02:30.904 --> 00:02:38.702
He traveled frequently between Alexandria and Cairo, and had little time for his beloved Suez Canal project.

24
00:02:38.703 --> 00:02:43.271
It was also difficult to get the ruler of Egypt to support his plans,

25
00:02:43.272 --> 00:02:49.388
even though Ferdinand was teaching French and horseback riding to the ruler's grandson.

26
00:02:49.389 --> 00:02:58.892
Still Ferdinand did not give up. He continued to study the height of the land and the water levels along possible routes for the canal.

27
00:02:58.893 --> 00:03:07.000
In 1837 Ferdinand was transferred from Egypt back to Europe to take up other diplomatic posts.

28
00:03:07.001 --> 00:03:13.196
He was sad to leave, and it was twenty years before he saw his beloved Egypt again.

29
00:03:13.197 --> 00:03:22.160
During those years, as his diplomatic responsibilities increased, his dream of the canal began to fade.

30
00:03:22.161 --> 00:03:29.431
In 1849 Ferdinand retired and went to live in a villa on the outskirts of Paris.

31
00:03:29.432 --> 00:03:37.561
It seemed to him that his great dream belonged to a part of his life that was over, but things were about to change.

32
00:03:37.562 --> 00:03:45.369
One morning in 1854, Ferdinand was reading the newspaper when a headline caught his eye.

33
00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:53.255
He read that Abbas Pasha, the ruler of Egypt, was dead and that Said Pasha had inherited the position.

34
00:03:53.256 --> 00:03:58.452
Said Pasha was the boy Ferdinand had taught back in Alexandria.

35
00:03:58.453 --> 00:04:04.597
The young prince was now a grown man who remembered the retired French consul fondly.

36
00:04:04.598 --> 00:04:10.214
Ferdinand knew that the new ruler of Egypt would support him in building the canal.

37
00:04:10.215 --> 00:04:13.592
His dream was coming to life once again.

38
00:04:13.593 --> 00:04:21.314
When Said invited Ferdinand to come to Egypt to celebrate his new position, Ferdinand promptly agreed.

39
00:04:21.315 --> 00:04:24.581
In the time since Ferdinand's first posting to Egypt,

40
00:04:24.582 --> 00:04:30.588
the reports about the different water levels of the Mediterranean and Red seas had been proved wrong.

41
00:04:30.589 --> 00:04:35.413
Now the only obstacle was finding the money to build the canal.

42
00:04:35.414 --> 00:04:43.821
Almost everyone thought Ferdinand was out of his mind to dig a canal in the desert, and he had a hard time finding financial supporters.

43
00:04:43.822 --> 00:04:49.404
But his diplomatic charm, optimism, and belief in the project prevailed.

44
00:04:49.405 --> 00:04:56.672
Ferdinand traveled between Europe and Egypt, gathering support for his project.

45
00:04:56.673 --> 00:05:06.562
It took him five years, but he was able to find supporters in France and Egypt who bought shares in his newly-formed Suez Canal Company.

46
00:05:06.563 --> 00:05:15.025
In 1859 he finally had enough funds to start digging; construction began in April of that year.

47
00:05:15.026 --> 00:05:22.146
Once construction started, it took ten years of hard labor to complete the canal.

48
00:05:22.147 --> 00:05:26.543
Said Pasha supplied Ferdinand with the many laborers he needed.

49
00:05:26.544 --> 00:05:31.299
A total of one and a half million laborers worked on the canal,

50
00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:40.581
mostly by hand, digging in the sandy soil in extremely hot weather with almost no machinery to aid them.

51
00:05:40.582 --> 00:05:50.150
Finally, after much hardship and the death of many laborers, the Suez Canal was completed in 1869.

52
00:05:50.151 --> 00:05:55.467
The day the Suez Canal officially opened, there was a grand celebration.

53
00:05:55.468 --> 00:06:02.320
Important people from all over the world came to Egypt, including Empress Eugenie of France.

54
00:06:02.321 --> 00:06:08.780
It had taken Ferdinand over forty years of hard work, but his dream had been realized.

55
00:06:08.781 --> 00:06:13.647
Europe and the Far East were finally connected.

56
00:06:13.648 --> 00:06:24.578
The Suez Canal, one of the longest in the world, is 166 kilometers long, 11 meters deep, and 60 meters wide.

57
00:06:24.579 --> 00:06:29.451
It can accommodate any ship except the largest supertankers.

58
00:06:29.452 --> 00:06:36.210
Each year thousands of ships from almost every country in the world travel through the canal.

59
00:06:36.211 --> 00:06:43.330
In fact, 14 percent of all the goods traded worldwide pass through this waterway.

60
00:06:43.331 --> 00:06:54.993
Ferdinand's dream to connect the world took him on a long and difficult journey, but in the end, the determined French diplomat succeeded.

